WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:03.570
Hey, what’s up?

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:04.570
I am Ryan Oksenberg.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.279
I’m a writer/director.

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:10.940
Right off the top I wanted to thank Film Courage
for giving me the opportunity to share with

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:15.210
you some directing lessons that I learned
making DAMAGE CONTROL.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:21.180
DAMAGE CONTROL is about a man who surprises
his fiancé with a new property and he’s

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.590
forced to confront his past behavior.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:30.130
It’s a classic morality play about karma
coming around in the vein of The Twilight

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.380
Zone.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.930
I’ve been making movies since I was a kid.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:40.129
I used to have one of those Indiglo stopwatches
and I would get all of my toys together and

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:44.579
create these epics and then I would look at
the watch, I’d hit stop and three hours

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:45.579
would have passed.

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:49.620
I would then go to a notepad and write down
what those stories were.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.140
I’ve always been engaged in making movies.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.489
I’ve made short films.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:00:58.120
I’ve made documentaries.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.040
I’ve shot things for non-profits (more of
like the corporate thing), the bread-and-butter

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.040
thing.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.610
But I’ve also always been writing.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.040
I’ve optioned scripts.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.720
I’ve been hired to adapt novels, I write
with people, I write for people.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.040
Directing is the most fun.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.450
That makes me feel the most alive.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.100
I love being on my feet.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:26.450
I love problem-solving in the moment and to
do that you’d think, I’m going to prepare.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.170
That night before your shoot, you prepare
like crazy.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:36.899
I just break down the shots, make my shot
list, I imagine myself in the location, I

00:01:36.899 --> 00:01:40.490
write down little things I’m going to whisper
in the actor’s ears.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:45.939
I’m a hundred and 10 percent prepared.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:52.040
But on DAMAGE CONTROL what I didn’t prepare
for was being unprepared.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:59.659
Now I wanted to ask you…have you ever been
in a situation, onset, where this unforeseeable

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:06.380
thing happens and now everything is in disarray
and you’re panicking because you may not

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.290
have your movie, you may not make your day.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:14.270
The biggest lesson that I’ve learned and
that I would say I am still learning is how

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.320
to be what I call a malleable director.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.880
A director that could roll with all the punches.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.160
You have to take what the film Gods throw
at you.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.410
It forces you any sort of restriction to be
creative and who knows what could come out

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:31.410
of it?

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.060
Maybe there’s a gift on the other end of
that?

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:42.020
The first thing that I learned on DAMAGE CONTROL
about being a malleable director is letting

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.560
go of the script.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:51.400
I had to show the whole history of a relationship
up until this moment in eight minutes.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:59.890
On set I realized that we didn’t have in
the writing what this relationship was because

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:06.170
we scrambled over in the writing not to have
any expository information about them, that’s

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.240
not interesting.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:13.760
What’s interesting is letting actors be
in the moment in play and convey to us what

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.870
the dialogue didn’t about who they are.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:24.410
Elia Kazan said that the directing is in the
casting and if you cast great people, that’s

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:27.580
who your character is.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:33.340
The next lesson on being a malleable director
is sale with the wind, not against it.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:41.390
We had 10 hours to shoot 10 pages over two
days and it was all in available light.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:47.910
So myself, the cinematographer Andy Chinn,
grip and electric Joseph Tan, we strategically

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:53.710
planned the order that we’re going to shoot
things because DAMAGE CONTROL takes place

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.440
in a single moment.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:02.580
It’s Alison’s lunch break when Drew surprises
her with this new property.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.810
The second day we shot out all the shack stuff.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:10.370
We reserved the entire morning for it and
we were early.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:14.850
It went over brilliantly but now we had an
hour and a half until lunch.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.431
So I was like to Andy “Let’s shoot this
now.

00:04:17.431 --> 00:04:20.880
Oh now it doesn’t match before, let’s
shoot this.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.820
The light’s not right, we’ve got to shoot
it later.”

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:28.940
And we were waisting time looking at the monitor
and seeing the shots that we shot yesterday

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:34.450
if they matched how the light looked now and
that would determine which shot we would set

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.760
up next and then I started worrying “Oh,
we have to move all of our gear this way and

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:40.760
then that way.”

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.880
I didn’t know what to shoot?

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.750
We spent a half hour talking about what we
could have done and what I neglected to do

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.210
was like “Okay, let’s shoot the inserts.”

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:56.880
So you have to learn just how to just throw
away your schedule and be a good editor.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.790
In your head you need to take tally of all
the things that you had shot up until then

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:07.710
and be able to piece it all together in your
head because now you are shooting it piecemeal.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:12.181
It’s just as important as a director is
now if you’re shooting things out of the

00:05:12.181 --> 00:05:17.780
order the actors anticipated, perhaps they
didn’t do the preparation for that scene

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.630
or they didn’t lead themselves up emotionally
to it, it’s up to you to be the compass

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:26.190
and guide them back to where they need to
be.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:32.280
Another phase in production where I learned
to be a more malleable director is in editing.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:39.930
In my rough cut I was just using medium closes
of Ana in the shack.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:47.120
I didn’t use any of the wides and I was
pretty committed to it.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.370
I loved the vulnerability that she could express
on her face without saying anything.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:06:00.250
I just had an idea of what she had been through
and what she is trying to communicate to Drew

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.540
in the shack.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:11.360
And what I did was I was cutting just to close-ups
of her and then what I was getting was a lack

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.000
of mystery and this came from the writer’s
note [Matthew Wygodny].

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.240
He was like “Keep Ana back.”

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:25.840
And I did and then it was like I’m going
to build up to that close-up shot, I’m going

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.100
to find the best moment to do it and I did
and it worked way better.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:42.540
You have to serve the story in the edit rather
than the shots you love or the movie that

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:49.860
you had in your head before or else you are
going to not work with the natural rhythms

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:58.310
of what this movie is and you are also going
to be pretty down on yourself because you

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:01.370
have this expectation of what it’s supposed
to be.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.330
But it’s really this so you have to make
this work.

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:07.830
I used to be pretty rigid making my movies.

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:09.620
I used to be pretty insular.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:14.520
I used to not share my rough cuts with friends.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:19.650
I used to be afraid to ask crew members “What
does this mean?”

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.060
Or “What does this do?”

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:28.710
And on DAMAGE CONTROL was the time where I
just completely let go and I’d like to hear

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:36.010
stories from you guys about gifts (great things)
that came out of problems and how you solve

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:42.620
them and how your movie and yourself as a
director benefited from them.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.830
I hope you guys found this video helpful.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:53.230
Talking about it is even helpful to me and
working out just how I approach directing

00:07:53.230 --> 00:08:02.020
and manage my expectations but when all of
those elements come together, when the performance

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:07.990
is spot-on, the camera movement is spot-on,
the sound is great, just everything is working

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:14.280
operatically and you’re the conductor of
it all, there is no other feeling like it.

